# tools/crypto_monocypher_sha512_parity_witness.rish — Monocypher-source parity for standalone SHA-512 (FIPS 180-4 / RFC 6234), the hash Ed25519 signs
# with and the hash every HMAC-SHA512, HKDF-SHA512, and BIP32 seed derivation folds its inputs through. Our authored crypto/sha512.rye and the vendored
# Monocypher's own C (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual), each compiled fresh and asked for the same five digests, diffed byte-for-byte. The TENTH rung of
# the real Monocypher-source parity pattern (BLAKE2b · X25519 · Ed25519 · IETF AEAD · Argon2 · XChaCha20-Poly1305 flagship · Edwards↔Montgomery ·
# standalone Poly1305 · standalone ChaCha20 came before). Season G · Cryptography.
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md — Season G's crux rungs name "the parity witness
# proving Rye output equals Monocypher output byte-for-byte." crypto/sha512.rye's own selftest named Monocypher-source parity as an honest held horizon
# ("Monocypher passes the same standard vectors — its vendored source is a held fetch"); this rung closes it. SHA-512 was proven EMBEDDED inside Ed25519
# signing, yet never STANDALONE against Monocypher's own crypto_sha512 — so the hash the whole signing line, the HMAC/HKDF-SHA512 family, and the BIP32
# seed all rest on now stands proven in its own right, the hash-cipher counterpart to the standalone ChaCha20 and Poly1305 rungs.
# Kin: crypto/sha512.rye (our authored hash, GREEN against Zig's std.crypto and the FIPS 180-4 vectors) · tools/monocypher_sha512_parity.rye (our
# printing harness) · tools/fixtures/monocypher_sha512_harness.c (Monocypher's printing harness) · tools/crypto_monocypher_ed25519_parity_witness.rish
# (the Ed25519 sibling that hashes THROUGH SHA-512 — this rung proves the hash beneath it standalone). Monocypher's SHA-512 lives in its OPTIONAL module
# (src/optional/monocypher-ed25519.c), linked so like meets like. Monocypher stays the parity TARGET, vendored and unmodified, never a copied line,
# studied clean-room (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# The five lines are anchored to the world twice over: line 1 is the FIPS 180-4 SHA-512 of the empty message (published), line 2 is the FIPS 180-4
# SHA-512 of "abc" (published); lines 3-5 are digests over deterministic inputs of sizes {111, 112, 300} — the two padding edges (length-fits-block and
# forces-second-block) and a multi-block input — where the oracle is a second real implementation agreeing byte-for-byte.
# Purely local — no key of ours, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/crypto_monocypher_sha512_parity_witness.rish

say "crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: Language EN — Monocypher-source parity for standalone SHA-512 (FIPS 180-4 / RFC 6234), the hash Ed25519 signs with and the hash every HMAC-SHA512, HKDF-SHA512, and BIP32 seed derivation folds its inputs through. crypto/sha512.rye named Monocypher-source parity as a held horizon and was only ever proven EMBEDDED inside Ed25519 signing; this tenth rung closes the horizon and proves the hash STANDALONE against the ACTUAL vendored Monocypher C over five digests, byte-for-byte."
say "crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: Style Radiant — like against like. Our authored hash and the unmodified vendored Monocypher each compile fresh and each print, in order, the SHA-512 of the empty message, of \"abc\", then digests over deterministic inputs of sizes {111, 112, 300}. Two independent implementations, one set of inputs; the parity is that the five lines agree hex for hex."
say "crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: Lens TAME — no fabricated constant is trusted: lines 1 and 2 are anchored to FIPS 180-4's published SHA-512 of the empty message and of \"abc\"; the padding-edge lines' oracle is a second real implementation. The witness diffs the five Monocypher lines against the five Rye lines byte-for-byte AND asserts both published anchors. Monocypher's SHA-512 is its optional module, linked never copied. Purely local — no key of ours, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays the named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let cbin = "crypto/bin/monocypher_sha512"
let cout = "crypto/bin/monocypher_sha512.out"
let rout = "crypto/bin/rye_sha512.out"

# 1. Build the Monocypher C harness, linking the unmodified vendored core AND the optional SHA-512 module with zig cc.
let cbuild = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && ${zig} cc -O2 -I vendor/monocypher/src -I vendor/monocypher/src/optional tools/fixtures/monocypher_sha512_harness.c vendor/monocypher/src/monocypher.c vendor/monocypher/src/optional/monocypher-ed25519.c -o ${cbin}"]
assert cbuild.ok else "crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: Monocypher C harness build failed — ${cbuild.err}"

# 2. Run the Monocypher C harness — five SHA-512 hex lines.
let crun = run ["sh" "-c" "${cbin} > ${cout} 2>&1"]
assert crun.ok else "crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: Monocypher C harness exited non-zero — see ${cout}"

# 3. Run our authored Rye harness — five SHA-512 hex lines over the identical inputs.
let rrun = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye run tools/monocypher_sha512_parity.rye > ${rout} 2>&1"]
assert rrun.ok else "crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: Rye harness exited non-zero — see ${rout}"

# 4. Byte-for-byte parity: the two five-line outputs must be identical.
let diff = run ["sh" "-c" "diff ${cout} ${rout} > /dev/null && echo same"]
assert (trim diff.out) == "same" else "crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: Monocypher and Rye digests disagree — Monocypher:\n$(cat ${cout})\nRye:\n$(cat ${rout})"

# 5. Anchor to the world: line 1 is FIPS 180-4's SHA-512 of the empty message, line 2 is the SHA-512 of "abc".
let fips_empty = "cf83e1357eefb8bdf1542850d66d8007d620e4050b5715dc83f4a921d36ce9ce47d0d13c5d85f2b0ff8318d2877eec2f63b931bd47417a81a538327af927da3e"
let fips_abc = "ddaf35a193617abacc417349ae20413112e6fa4e89a97ea20a9eeee64b55d39a2192992a274fc1a836ba3c23a3feebbd454d4423643ce80e2a9ac94fa54ca49f"

let got_empty = run ["sh" "-c" "sed -n '1p' ${cout}"]
assert (trim got_empty.out) == fips_empty else "crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: line 1 is not the FIPS 180-4 published SHA-512 of the empty message"

let got_abc = run ["sh" "-c" "sed -n '2p' ${cout}"]
assert (trim got_abc.out) == fips_abc else "crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: line 2 is not the FIPS 180-4 published SHA-512 of \"abc\""

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${cout}"]
say "crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: the five lines, agreed by both implementations:\n${shown.out}"

say "GREEN crypto-monocypher-sha512-parity: our authored crypto/sha512.rye and the vendored Monocypher's own C, each compiled fresh, produce byte-for-byte identical SHA-512 output over the empty message and \"abc\" (both anchored to their FIPS 180-4 published known-answers) and over the padding-edge sizes {111, 112, 300} (length-fits-block · forces-second-block · multi-block). The TENTH real Monocypher-SOURCE parity rung, closing the held horizon crypto/sha512.rye itself named and proving the standalone hash Ed25519 signs with — the hash every HMAC-SHA512, HKDF-SHA512, and BIP32 seed derivation rests on — the hash-cipher counterpart to the standalone ChaCha20 and Poly1305 rungs. Monocypher's SHA-512 is its optional module, linked never copied. Purely local, no key of ours, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays the named horizon."
